[Backblaze B2](https://www.backblaze.com/cloud-storage) is S3-compatible cloud object storage from Backblaze. Every bucket has an S3 endpoint, such as `s3.us-west-004.backblazeb2.com`, whose middle part names the region the bucket lives in.

# How does the integration work?

By default, self-hosted Appwrite Storage keeps uploaded files on your server's local disk. The `backblaze` storage adapter stores them in a B2 bucket instead, so file storage scales separately from your server and survives a server rebuild. Appwrite builds the bucket address, `<bucket>.s3.<region>.backblazeb2.com`, from the bucket name and region you configure.

{% info title="Self-hosted only" %}
Storage adapters configure a self-hosted Appwrite instance. Appwrite Cloud manages file storage for you.
{% /info %}

# How to implement

To store Appwrite files in Backblaze B2, there are several steps you must complete:

## Step 1: Create a bucket and an application key

In your Backblaze account, create a private B2 bucket. Note the bucket name and the region from its **Endpoint**, for example `us-west-004` from `s3.us-west-004.backblazeb2.com`.

Then create an application key that can read and write the bucket. Save the **keyID**, which Appwrite uses as the access key, and the **applicationKey**, which Appwrite uses as the secret. Backblaze shows the application key only once.

## Step 2: Configure the Backblaze adapter in Appwrite

For this step, you must [self-host Appwrite](/docs/advanced/self-hosting) if you haven't already.

Open the `.env` file of your Appwrite installation and set the storage variables:

```env
_APP_STORAGE_DEVICE=backblaze
_APP_STORAGE_S3_ACCESS_KEY=<KEY_ID>
_APP_STORAGE_S3_SECRET=<APPLICATION_KEY>
_APP_STORAGE_S3_REGION=us-west-004
_APP_STORAGE_S3_BUCKET=<BUCKET_NAME>
```

`_APP_STORAGE_DEVICE=backblaze` selects the Backblaze adapter, and the `_APP_STORAGE_S3_*` variables hold its credentials, region, and bucket.

{% info title="Upgrading an older configuration" %}
Older installations set `_APP_STORAGE_BACKBLAZE_ACCESS_KEY`, `_APP_STORAGE_BACKBLAZE_SECRET`, `_APP_STORAGE_BACKBLAZE_REGION`, and `_APP_STORAGE_BACKBLAZE_BUCKET`. Appwrite 2.0 and later still read them as a fallback when `_APP_STORAGE_S3_ACCESS_KEY`, `_APP_STORAGE_S3_SECRET`, and `_APP_STORAGE_S3_BUCKET` are not all set, but they are deprecated, and the Open Runtimes orchestrator that builds Functions and Sites doesn't read them. Move the values to the `_APP_STORAGE_S3_*` variables.
{% /info %}

Recreate the containers and check that the variables are applied:

```bash
docker compose up -d
docker compose exec appwrite vars
```

Changing the storage device doesn't move files Appwrite has already stored, so configure it before you store production files.

## Step 3: Test the adapter

Open your project in the Appwrite Console, go to **Storage**, create a bucket, and upload a file. The file then appears in your B2 bucket in the Backblaze web UI.

